WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.747
Qdrant moves us closer to a full vector database.

00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:08.337
A Qdrant collection stores vectors together with payload metadata.

00:00:08.337 --> 00:00:13.342
We specify the vector dimension and the distance metric, then insert points.

00:00:13.342 --> 00:00:17.337
Each point has an ID, a vector, and a payload.

00:00:17.337 --> 00:00:22.792
The payload can include fields such as title, topic, source, permission,

00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:25.532
or any other metadata useful for filtering.

00:00:25.532 --> 00:00:33.932
At query time, we embed the question and ask Qdrant for the nearest points.

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:40.062
The classroom example can run in memory, but Qdrant can also run as a persistent server or managed

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:45.802
cloud service. What should feel familiar is the information flow.

00:00:45.802 --> 00:00:49.427
We still have chunk text, metadata, embeddings, and a query vector.

00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:56.452
The database simply gives us more infrastructure around those pieces, especially filtering, persistence,

00:00:56.452 --> 00:00:59.200
and service-style deployment.
